# mutgap_mem_tracker_compact_empty_block: Compaction next to an emptied run block kills the host. As in tasks_gap_tracker_empty_block, a full block of 240 runs from page 100, a block at page 700, a run at 650 that splits the first, and a map of [640, 701) that removes 700's run from the last block, which stays in the list, empty; one more run at page 101 makes block 0 hold 121. An empty block compares Greater than everything, so the 15,119 one-page runs that follow, at every other page from 702, all go to the block before it, which splits each time it is full (the comparison is Greater there) until there are 128 blocks. The next run finds that block full and 128 blocks, and compact packs the blocks from the front; their total is not a multiple of 240, so the last non-empty block is left short with the empty block after it, and the steal of nothing asserts (compact asserts it steals at least one run): the host dies at that map. Blocks only; one task of 15 x 4096 pages, every leaf one RAM page (newops_util.AliasedTask), the runs written with mem_seq into the largest root FIFO.
